<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  `ld` — the first two letters of the name.

  Both are drawn with a ruler rather than taken from a font. The first version used
  the real DejaVu Sans Mono Bold `l`, and it was wrong on two counts: that glyph has
  a curved tail and a flag at the top, which at icon size reads as a smudge, and a
  filled glyph beside a stroked one does not render the same weight even when the
  numbers match. Both letters are now single-weight strokes and both are straight.
  This is a terminal's letterform, which is what the app is.

  The pair still obeys the font's proportions, which is what stops it looking like two
  drawings: the stroke is the font's stem width (293 units) with a ~10% optical
  correction for being a stroke rather than a fill, both letters share the font's
  baseline (0) and ascender (1556), and the second letter's bowl top is the font's
  x-height (1120 units) — measured off the `x` glyph, because `o` overshoots and this
  font's `OS/2` table is version 1 with no x-height field in it at all.

  The `d` is deliberately sharp: a chevron opening left for the bowl, with a bar
  running from the ascender down to the baseline for the stem. It is drawn as a
  chevron on purpose and should not be rounded or squared off.

  Everything sits inside the middle 66% so no shape of icon mask can clip it. The
  group scale is what keeps it there: the letters' own ink came to 71.3 x 49.8 in a
  108 viewport, which is the safe zone exactly, so the mark filled the icon edge to
  edge and read as too heavy. Scaling the group rather than re-deriving coordinates
  means the strokes scale with the geometry and the proportions cannot drift.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <group
        android:pivotX="54"
        android:pivotY="54"
        android:scaleX="0.72"
        android:scaleY="0.72">
        <!-- the l: one straight stem, ascender to baseline, no tail and no flag -->
        <path
            android:pathData="M32.56,29.09 L32.56,78.91"
            android:strokeColor="#EDEDED"
            android:strokeWidth="10.4"
            android:strokeLineCap="butt"
            android:strokeLineJoin="miter" />
        <!-- the sharp d: the ascender, the bowl, and back up the stem, as ONE path.

             It was two strokes, a stem and a chevron, and their endpoints coincided
             at the stem's tip (84.95, 78.91). Two butt-capped strokes ending at the
             same point do not make a corner: each stops in a square face
             perpendicular to its own direction, so the join leaves a notch. Drawing
             it as a single path makes that corner a real miter join, which is the
             only way this can be right.

             The path closes back at the ascender's top, where the two segments are
             collinear and the join degenerates to a clean square end. -->
        <path
            android:pathData="M84.95,29.09 L84.95,43.05 L62.53,60.98 L84.95,78.91 L84.95,29.09"
            android:strokeColor="#EDEDED"
            android:strokeWidth="10.4"
            android:strokeLineCap="butt"
            android:strokeLineJoin="miter"
            android:strokeMiterLimit="3" />
    </group>
</vector>
